WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.198
Many secret leaks in Go services are not caused by a secret store failure. They are caused by ordinary developer convenience.

2
00:00:08.198 --> 00:00:16.162
A fmt.Printf call added during debugging prints a full config struct. A structured log records an Authorization header.

3
00:00:16.162 --> 00:00:23.658
A wrapped error includes a database DSN. A panic dumps a request object. A test fixture contains a realistic token.

4
00:00:23.658 --> 00:00:31.544
A golden file preserves output that included credentials. A JSON response accidentally serializes an internal field.

5
00:00:31.544 --> 00:00:39.039
These are small local decisions that become durable exposure paths. The rule is not simply do not log the password.

6
00:00:39.039 --> 00:00:47.472
Modern services carry secrets in shapes that do not look like a variable named password. A DSN can include username and password.

7
00:00:47.472 --> 00:00:55.670
An HTTP Authorization header can contain a bearer token. A webhook signature secret may be part of a verifier configuration.

8
00:00:55.670 --> 00:01:01.292
A private key may be loaded as PEM bytes. A cloud credential may be a structured object.

9
00:01:01.292 --> 00:01:08.397
Logging the entire object because it is convenient can expose the secret even when no one typed log password.

10
00:01:08.397 --> 00:01:13.082
Redaction should be a design pattern, not a heroic last-minute filter.

11
00:01:13.082 --> 00:01:19.328
Secret-bearing types can implement String carefully so fmt does not reveal sensitive fields.

12
00:01:19.328 --> 00:01:23.623
Logging helpers can accept safe summaries instead of raw config.

13
00:01:23.623 --> 00:01:30.494
Structs intended for JSON should avoid exporting secret fields, or should use explicit redacted views.

14
00:01:30.494 --> 00:01:38.926
Errors should identify what failed, such as database connection setup failed, without embedding the credential that was used.

15
00:01:38.926 --> 00:01:48.530
When context is needed, include nonsecret identifiers such as environment, service name, host alias, or provider key name, not the value itself.

16
00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:56.572
Tests deserve the same discipline. Use fake values that cannot unlock real systems. Keep .env files out of source control.

17
00:01:56.572 --> 00:02:00.164
Avoid copying real production headers into fixtures.

18
00:02:00.164 --> 00:02:08.206
If a test needs to prove redaction works, assert that the output contains REDACTED and does not contain the original value.

19
00:02:08.206 --> 00:02:17.966
This turns leak prevention into a behavior the codebase checks. In Go, you cannot rely on every future caller to remember which fields are dangerous.

20
00:02:17.966 --> 00:02:26.788
Build safer defaults into the types and helpers they use. If printing a config object is unsafe, make the printed representation safe.

21
00:02:26.788 --> 00:02:32.488
If logs need request metadata, provide a function that extracts only approved fields.

22
00:02:32.488 --> 00:02:42.560
Good redaction does not make secrets harmless, but it reduces the number of accidental paths where a routine debug action becomes a credential incident.

23
00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:45.762
Reviewers should look for indirect logging too.

24
00:02:45.762 --> 00:02:54.585
A helper may not log Password directly, but it may log a request, config, URL, or error that contains the password inside another field.

25
00:02:54.585 --> 00:03:02.783
The safest pattern is to design safe views for diagnostics. Give the logger only the fields that are approved for disclosure.

26
00:03:02.783 --> 00:03:09.185
When the unsafe value never reaches the logging call, redaction does not depend on memory or luck.
